{
  "id": "NP04Z",
  "version": "2.0",
  "title": "Spinal cord stimulator implantation",
  "description": "A spinal cord stimulator is a device that sends mild electrical pulses to your spinal cord to block or reduce chronic pain signals before they reach your brain. Your surgeon places electrodes near your spinal cord and connects them to a pulse generator implanted under your skin, usually near your buttock or abdomen. You'll likely have a trial period with temporary leads to confirm the device will help your pain before permanent implantation. The outpatient procedure takes 1-3 hours depending on complexity, and you can usually go home the same day. Recovery typically takes 1-2 weeks before you can resume normal activities. The device requires periodic programming adjustments and battery replacement every few years. The cost of this procedure may vary depending on the complexity involved. Categories include revision/removal, trial/temporary electrodes, simple, trial/temporary electrodes, plate/paddle, and implantation, pulse generator / definitive.",
